P8326:
Williams proposed a natural gas power plant in Eagle Mountain, Utah, and a pipeline to supply it. The company called the project Aquila.{mw} Its subsidiary Will-Power UT LLC would build and own the pipeline. In a November 2025 filing with the Utah Public Service Commission, the company said it was still getting permits.{psc}

Williams described the pipeline as 19 miles long and running from the MountainWest Pipeline.{mw} The commission filing gives the length as about 20 miles.{psc} The company's spring 2026 slides gave a 12.5 year agreement. The plans called for the power plant to start in two phases, in the second half of 2027 and the first half of 2028.{mw}

A February 2026 hearing notice from Eagle Mountain said Williams wanted to build a gas power plant next to the Meta data center expansion. The notice did not mention the Aquila name or the pipeline.{emc}

P8222:
In February 2009, Oasis Pipeline LP and Oasis Pipe Line Company Texas LP applied to the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission (FERC) to build border-crossing facilities.{fr} A June 2009 news report says FERC approved them.{ngi}

The Clint Export Project would have carried gas about 188 miles from the Waha Hub in West Texas to near Clint, in El Paso County. The pipe would be 36 inches wide and carry about 600 million cubic feet per day. The gas would have gone on to the Tarahumara Pipeline in Chihuahua, Mexico. The Texas part of the project also needed approval from the state regulator.{ngi}

No report of construction, a start date, or a formal cancellation has been found. The project is treated as cancelled because no work has been reported since 2009.

P8096:
WBI Energy Transmission proposed the Bakken East Pipeline in North Dakota. It would run about 374 miles from near Watford City to the company's compressor station near Mapleton.{wbi} The pipeline would be built in two phases and carry about 1 billion cubic feet per day.{ogw} The company planned the first phase for November 2029 and the second for November 2030.{ogw}

The company held a binding open season in March 2026 and began the pre-filing process with the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission.{wbi} In April 2026 North Dakota confirmed state financing of up to $500 million for the project.{ogw}

The company told state officials that many interested customers were developing power generation to support data centers.{ndm}

P8259:
Dominion Energy North Carolina proposed the T-15 Reliability Project, a 45-mile pipeline between Eden and Roxboro that would run beside an existing pipeline right of way.{ncn} The line would run within a single state, so it would be regulated by the North Carolina Utilities Commission and would have no federal filing.

The state approved a water quality certification on 16 September 2025, and 2026 permits covered construction sites for a compressor station and a metering station.{deq} The pipeline has since come under the ownership of Enbridge.{av} No source confirms that pipe is in the ground, so the status of construction is an inference from these permits.

The pipeline would supply a new Duke Energy gas plant in Person County, which was designed in part for expected data center demand.{cm}

P4457:
NextEra Energy Resources proposed the Lowman Pipeline to supply gas to the Lowman Energy Center, a new gas-fired power plant (see [[Charles R. Lowman Power Plant]]) owned by PowerSouth Energy Cooperative in Washington County, Alabama. A July 2023 copy of the project website describes a proposed pipeline of about 51 miles that would receive gas from existing pipelines in Choctaw County and run south to the plant. It would also need one compressor station in Choctaw County. The website says a subsidiary of NextEra Energy Resources would develop, build, own and operate the pipeline.{lowco}

The pipeline receives gas from two interstate pipelines in Choctaw County, Midcontinent Express Pipeline and Gulf South Pipeline. A May 2020 permit application to the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers describes a 53.75-mile, 16-inch line with interconnects at both pipelines, one compressor station, three meter stations and a launcher and receiver facility.{corps20} A local newspaper report from the construction period says the line taps the existing Gulf South and Midcontinent Express pipelines and starts about eight miles west of Butler.{clarke} An October 2019 request to the Corps, made before the route was set, instead placed the start near Transco's Station 85 in Choctaw County and listed four meter stations.{corps19} A trade profile of the plant names Kinder Morgan and Gulf South as the suppliers.{nsenergy}

PowerSouth and Mitsubishi Power announced on October 23, 2023 that the Lowman Energy Center had begun commercial operations. The plant replaced the retired Charles R. Lowman coal-fired plant.{mhilec} NextEra Energy Resources now describes the Lowman Pipeline as an approximately 54-mile intrastate pipeline that carries natural gas from interconnects near Butler to the plant in Leroy, Alabama.{nextera}

P8334:
Gazprom Gazoraspredelenie Krasnodar commissioned this inter-settlement gas pipeline in October 2025, according to an operator press release dated 9 October 2025. The pipeline runs from Apsheronsk through Kalinina, Vpered and Yerik to Kubanskaya and Tverskaya in Krasnodar Krai.{w86}

The pipeline was built under Gazprom's program for developing gas supply and gasification of Krasnodar Krai for 2021 to 2025. A ceremony was held at the gas distribution station in Vpered during the St. Petersburg International Gas Forum 2025. The release said 19.3 kilometers of distribution networks had already been laid from the pipeline. It said the pipeline allows gas to reach five settlements.{w86} A Kuban news report that relays the regional administration press service gave the same length and the same five settlements.{w87}

The sources do not give the diameter, pressure, capacity, cost or construction year. They also do not say which network feeds the pipeline at Apsheronsk.

P8338:
Gazprom is building a gas pipeline to the Erzi children's camp in the Dzheirakh district of Ingushetia under its 2021 to 2025 gasification program. The program was signed by Gazprom and the head of Ingushetia in November 2020. In April 2023 it was updated to add the reconstruction of the Dzheirakh gas distribution station.{w88}

In May 2025 the head of Gazprom Mezhregiongaz discussed the line with the head of Ingushetia at the Caucasus Investment Forum. A regional press service statement from January 2026 gave the length as 43.2 kilometers.{w89} A January 2021 report had given 52 kilometers for the Dzheirakh district as a whole. Completion of construction and assembly work was planned for 2027.

No tender, contract or start of construction has been found. No source states where the pipeline begins.

P8342:
Gazprom completed a high-pressure gas pipeline from the Karachaevsk gas distribution station to the city of Teberda, in Karachay-Cherkessia, in September 2021. A Gazprom statement from September 2022 gave its length as 25.9 kilometers and said it serves more than 4,000 households in Teberda, Nizhnyaya Teberda and Verkhnyaya Teberda.{w90} The head of the republic said in November 2021 that a line of about 26 kilometers had been commissioned in September.{w91}

An Interfax report from December 2019 said Gazprom began the second phase of the work in August 2018.{w92} The same report gave the whole Karachaevsk to Teberda line as 46.5 kilometers. It said the first phase, built in 2012 and 2013, was 22.5 kilometers.

The sources do not give the diameter, capacity, pressure or cost. No route was found.

P8347:
Gazprom gazoraspredelenie Syktyvkar built a high-pressure inter-settlement gas pipeline in the Komi Republic. It runs from the Krutyanskaya gas distribution station in the Ukhta district to Voivozh in the Sosnogorsk district. A 2022 operator release announced the start of construction and gave the length as 102 kilometers. It said the line would replace an emergency line on the Ukhta municipal balance sheet and supply 11 settlements with about 8,000 people. Completion was planned for 2022.{w93}

In September 2022, regional news reports said 84 of the 102 kilometers had been built, including horizontal directional drilling river crossings.{w94} A July 2024 report on the head of Komi's video address listed this line among the gas pipelines completed in 2023.{w95}

No source gives an exact commissioning date. The diameter, capacity and cost were not found, and no source states who holds title to the pipeline.

P8355:
In April 2026 Kommersant reported, citing the Gazprom transgaz Ukhta press service, that construction was complete on a gas pipeline branch 32.4 kilometers long and on the Nyandoma gas distribution station. The station has a capacity of 24.3 thousand cubic meters per hour. The branch belongs to the Nyuksenitsa to Arkhangelsk gas pipeline system and was built under the 2021 to 2025 gasification program.{w99}

The branch serves the Nyandoma, Konosha and Kargopol districts. It is operated by the Nyuksenitsa line production department. The station was tested under load and described as ready for operation. A trade news site republished the same press release.{w100}

The sources do not give the point where the branch joins the main line, or its diameter, pressure or cost. No route was found.

P8359:
A Gazprom news item from 16 August 2011 reported that the first stage of the Pochinki to Saransk gas pipeline was commissioned near Blokhino in the Lyambirsky district of Mordovia. The stage was 76 kilometers long with a pressure of 5.4 megapascals. Construction began in August 2010. The line branches off the Urengoy to Pomary to Uzhgorod corridor. Gazprom was the investor, and the Pochinkovskoe line production department of Gazprom transgaz Nizhny Novgorod operates it.{w101}

A page from the construction company Stroygazmontazh describes the work as the reconstruction of the Saratov to Gorky gas pipeline, Pochinki to Saransk section, in two launch complexes. It gives a length of 206.8 kilometers and a diameter of 720 millimeters. It says the new line replaced a worn pipeline from 1960.{w102} A 2025 page from the operator's Penza line production department lists the Pochinki to Saransk trunk line in its service area.{w103}

The capacity and cost were not found.

P8363:
A Gazprom press release of 31 July 2020 reported that gas was launched at Merkushi in Perm Krai. The investor was Gazprom Mezhregiongaz, and Gazprom Gazoraspredelenie Perm built and operates the line.{w104}

The Perm Krai gasification program for 2017 to 2021 lists the pipeline for 2018 to 2020. It runs from Shlyki through Nizhnee Gorodishche, Podzemlyannaya, Malo-Baydino, Verkhnyaya Davydovka, Monastyrka and Bogomyagkovo, with a branch to Merkushi. The program gives its length as 58.0 kilometers and lists it under the Osa and Chastye districts.{w105} A Perm newspaper published the same release text.{w106}

It was not confirmed whether a gas distribution station or an existing distribution main sits at Shlyki. The diameter, capacity, pressure and cost were not found.

P8367:
LUKOIL proposed a gas pipeline from the Stavrolen petrochemical site to Nevinnomyssk in Stavropol Krai. The pipeline would carry North Caspian gas into the Gazprom gas transmission system. The developer is LUKOIL-Nizhnevolzhskneft-Kalmykia, and the designer is Gipvostokneft.{w107}

On 8 November 2023 Neftegaz.ru, quoting a LUKOIL press release, reported a positive Glavgosekspertiza conclusion on the design documents and surveys. The pipeline would be just over 222 kilometers long and would cross the Budyonnovsk, Novoselitsky, Aleksandrovsky, Mineralovodsky, Andropovsky and Kochubeevsky districts before reaching Nevinnomyssk. A booster compressor station is planned near Stavrolen.{w107}

Russian Government order 816-r also lists a gas pipeline from Stavrolen to the Nevinnomyssk area. It gives a capacity of about 4.4 billion cubic meters a year and a 35 megawatt compressor station.{w108} No start of construction or final investment decision was verified. The diameter, pressure and cost were not found, and no published route was found.

P8371:
Gazprom group companies proposed a high-pressure inter-settlement gas pipeline from Uren to Vetluga in the north of Nizhny Novgorod Oblast. It is part of the regional gasification program agreed with Gazprom. The regional energy minister gave the length as 78 kilometers in December 2023.{w109} The same figure was repeated at a committee hearing in January 2026.{w110}

In January 2026 Gazprom Mezhregiongaz Nizhny Novgorod said a contract had been concluded for a 75 kilometer pipeline to Vetluga. The report did not say who the contract was with or whether it covered design or construction. Construction timing was to follow once the 2026 to 2030 program was approved.{w111} Reports gave construction in 2027 and commissioning in 2027 to 2028.{w110}

The diameter, capacity, cost and final investment decision were not stated. No route was found.

P8336:
Gazprom Transgaz Chaikovsky rebuilt two branch lines from Chusovoy through Berezniki to Solikamsk in Perm Krai. Work started in the summer of 2012. The company built two new strings of about 180 km each beside the old strings from 1970 and 1986, then dismantled the old pipe.{w155}

The second string was fully in service by mid-2015, and consumers were switched over in July 2015. The first string was in service for its first 160 km, and the last 20 km were due in the first half of 2016. A Kommersant report from March 2016 said both strings worked in full and all consumers were connected.{w156} The line supplies Berezniki, Solikamsk, Gubakha, Kizel, Yayva and Aleksandrovsk.

The contractor's project page gives 371 km for the two strings together. It says the first string was built in 2015 and main work on the second was completed in 2017.{w157} The contract was reported at about 17.5 billion rubles for the whole two-string rebuild.{w158}

P8365:
Gazprom added a gas line from Solikamsk toward Cherdyn in northern Perm Krai to its Perm Krai gasification programme. A Kommersant report of 2 September 2022 said an agreement of June 2022 brought it into the programme.{w169} A January 2024 report said the pipe was under construction and the first 3 km had been laid.{w170}

The contractor, Severnaya Kompaniya, lists the inter-settlement pipeline at 104.5 km.{w171} An RBC Perm report of December 2025 said the pipeline was longer than 100 km.{w172} The 209 km figure in some reports includes distribution networks.

In September 2026 reports said gas had reached Cherdyn, Gubdor, Seregovo, Berezovaya Starica and Ryabinino.{w173}{w174} Later stages to Krasnovishersk and beyond Cherdyn were announced in January 2024, with no lengths given.{w175}

P8333:
Gazprom is the sponsor of the Anapa to Taman expansion, a second string laid parallel to the existing Anapa to Taman line. Gazprom Invest is the customer, Gazprom Proektirovanie the designer, Gazstroyprom the contractor and Gazprom Transgaz Krasnodar the operator.{w185} A positive state expert review conclusion for the first stage, 16.7 kilometers long, was issued on 29 May 2023.

In a February 2025 press release, Gazprom Transgaz Krasnodar said laying of the second string had begun, with earthworks and welding under way. It gave the project capacity as 7.5 billion cubic meters a year and described it as covering kilometers 0 to 104.{w154} The project is also item 207 in the federal territorial planning scheme, in the version of 17 December 2025, at 7.5 billion cubic meters a year.{w108}

This entry covers the section from kilometer 16.0 to kilometer 104.1, which ends at the OTEKO industrial park in Temryuk District. The first stage is a separate piece. No pipe diameter, cost or route was found.
